.TH QFW-SLURM-DRIVER 1 "September 2026" "qfw-slurm 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
qfw-slurm-driver \- exercise QFw gateway reservation operations
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qfw-slurm-driver
.B evaluate
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B qfw-slurm-driver
.B reserve
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B qfw-slurm-driver
.B get-reservations
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B qfw-slurm-driver
.B release
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B qfw-slurm-driver
.B lifecycle
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B qfw-slurm-driver
is a Slurm-independent diagnostic frontend for the same typed gateway client
and reservation operations used by the Slurm lifecycle helper.
It does not load the SPANK module and does not link with libslurm.
.PP
The caller supplies allocation metadata that Slurm normally supplies to the
plugin. A production gateway verifies that metadata against an active job in
slurmctld before contacting QPM services.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B evaluate
Perform one non-binding admission evaluation. No reservation ID is created.
.TP
.B reserve
Request the configured QPM service set, validate every result, and print the
canonical reservation tuple set. The reservation remains active.
.TP
.B get-reservations
Retrieve the accepted tuple set for an observed active Slurm job. The gateway
derives its canonical allocation ID and reads the journal without contacting
the directory service or QPMd.
.TP
.B release
Release the reservation set associated with the cluster and canonical job ID.
Every service result is inspected even when an earlier result is unresolved.
.TP
.B lifecycle
Reserve, optionally hold, and release in one invocation. SIGINT and SIGTERM
trigger one bounded release attempt after an accepted reservation.
.SH ALLOCATION OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --config " path"
Read the protected native client and resource mapping from
.IR path .
.TP
.BI --cluster " name"
Set the Slurm cluster name expected by the gateway.
.TP
.BI --job-id " id"
Set the canonical Slurm job ID. For
.BR get-reservations ,
set the observed job or heterogeneous component ID instead.
.TP
.BI --uid " uid"
Set the job owner's numeric user ID.
.TP
.BI --gid " gid"
Set the job owner's numeric group ID.
.TP
.BI --allocation-epoch " seconds"
Set a stable allocation timestamp used in request identity. It is not required
by
.BR get-reservations .
.TP
.BI --walltime-seconds " seconds"
Set the finite reservation limit. Required by
.B reserve
and
.BR lifecycle .
.TP
.BI --hetero-job-id " id"
Identify one heterogeneous job component. Must be paired with
.BR --hetero-component .
.TP
.BI --hetero-component " index"
Set the heterogeneous component index.
.SH WORKLOAD OPTIONS
The following options are required by
.B reserve
and
.B lifecycle
unless stated otherwise.
.TP
.BI --qpu " name[,name...]"
Select one or more configured resource names. Each name maps to one exact QPM
service ID in
.BR qfw-slurm-plugin.conf (5).
.TP
.BI --workload-kind " kind"
Set
.B quantum
or
.BR hybrid .
.TP
.BI --circ-count " count"
Set the maximum circuit count.
.TP
.BI --max-qubits " count"
Set the maximum qubits per circuit.
.TP
.BI --max-depth " count"
Set the maximum circuit depth.
.TP
.BI --max-shots " count"
Set the maximum shots.
.TP
.BI --max-one-q-gates " count"
Set the optional maximum one-qubit gate count.
.TP
.BI --max-two-q-gates " count"
Set the optional maximum two-qubit gate count.
.TP
.BI --max-measurements " count"
Set the optional maximum measurement count.
.SH OTHER OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --release-reason " code"
Set the unsigned release reason. The default is zero.
.TP
.BI --hold-seconds " seconds"
For
.BR lifecycle ,
wait after acceptance before sending release.
.TP
.B --json
Emit one bounded JSON Lines record per operation instead of human-readable
output.
.TP
.B --help
Print the command summary.
.SH OUTPUT
Successful human-readable reserve output includes a shell command of the form
.PP
.RS
.B export QFW_RESERVATIONS='[["service-id","41"]]'
.RE
.PP
Reservation IDs are decimal strings so JSON preserves the complete unsigned
64-bit range. The driver never prints MUNGE credentials or provider secrets.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The requested command reached its expected terminal state.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command or argument.
.TP
.B 3
Transport failure or timeout.
.TP
.B 4
MUNGE or peer authentication failure.
.TP
.B 5
Gateway or QPM operation failure.
.TP
.B 6
Delayed or rejected admission.
.TP
.B 7
Invalid or incomplete normal response.
.TP
.B 8
Unresolved release.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/qfw-slurm/plugin.conf
Default protected gateway-client and resource mapping used by site commands.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR qfw-slurm-plugin.conf (5),
.BR qfw-slurm (7),
.BR qfw-slurm-gateway (8),
.BR qfw-slurm-bb (8)
